{
  "headline": "Fire at GRIDCo's Mallam Substation disrupts power in parts of Accra, Ghana",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "A fire outbreak at the Ghana Grid Company's (GRIDCo) Mallam Substation has disrupted power supply to parts of Accra, according to a press statement issued by the company's Corporate Communications Section on Wednesday, October 14, 2026. GRIDCo reported that a fault occurred at the substation at 2:36 a.m. that day, resulting in a fire on one of the station's transformers. The Ghana National Fire Service (GNFS) responded to the incident and successfully contained and extinguished the fire. The GNFS indicated that communities including Lapaz, Anyaa, and Awoshie are likely to be affected by the power disruption. GRIDCo stated that power supply had been restored to parts of the affected areas, while efforts to restore supply to the remaining areas are ongoing. The company's engineers are currently investigating the cause of the fire, and a process has been initiated to replace the affected transformer."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Dalit women in Bangladesh seek security to contest elections",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "At a roundtable discussion in Dhaka, Bangladesh, on October 11, 2026, Dalit women identified several obstacles preventing them from becoming election candidates. Participants, including Sonamoni Modak from Keraniganj and Sathi Sarkar, stated that insecurity and a lack of support are primary concerns. Modak expressed interest in contesting a reserved seat for women in future local government elections but noted that influential local candidates often use intimidation to discourage such attempts. Sarkar added that Dalit women cannot contest elections without assurances of security and support. Other women, Lipi Sarkar, Tithi Mondol, and Shampa Rani Das, cited insecurity, lack of political party support, family opposition, and derogatory societal remarks as barriers to candidacy. The event, titled “Dalit women’s rights in local government elections: Challenges and the way forward,” was organized by the non-governmental organization Avijan and Prothom Alo, with support from the Government of Sweden and technical assistance from the International Foundation for Electoral Systems (IFES). A keynote paper presented at the discussion estimated the Dalit population in Bangladesh to be between 5.5 million and 6.5 million people across more than 50 communities, noting that these groups face discrimination based on ancestry and occupation."
    },
    {
      "text": "During the discussion, Prime Minister’s Political Adviser Zahir Uddin Swapon stated the government is working toward an inclusive political system and urged Dalit communities to present their needs as demands within government initiatives. Prime Minister’s Special Assistant Bijon Kanti Sarkar noted that while past local government elections were influenced by a particular political party, the government has decided future local elections will not be held on a party basis. Sarkar stated there is no legal barrier to participation and that Dalit women should be able to participate without fear. Three women MPs from the Bangladesh Nationalist Party (BNP) also spoke at the event. Newaz Halima Arly cautioned against using disadvantaged Dalit women merely as a vote bank, while Madhabi Marma suggested that opportunities to contest local elections should be provided based on the population of Dalit women in respective areas and emphasized the need to change patriarchal attitudes."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Syrian Presidential Adviser calls for constitutional recognition of Kurdish rights",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Speaking at the MERI Forum 2026, Syrian Presidential Adviser Mazloum Abdi stated on Tuesday that Syria has entered a new phase and pledged to work toward the constitutional recognition of Kurdish rights. Abdi said that agreements reached with the Syrian presidency should be included in the constitution. He noted that Syria faces challenges in establishing a new governance system following the end of Baath rule and emphasized that all components of Syrian society should participate in building the country's future. According to Abdi, Kurdish representation in government ministries remains below expectations, and the status of the Kurdish language still needs to be addressed. He called on Kurdish political forces to build on ground-level gains, stating these gains belong to all Syrians rather than a single party. Abdi added that while agreements with the Syrian state were signed in the name of the Syrian Democratic Forces (SDF), they represent the Kurdish people as a whole."
    },
    {
      "text": "Abdi reported that an integration process has been underway for nine months, during which some SDF personnel have joined the Syrian army. He stated that military forces and security personnel are now part of the Syrian army, intelligence, and various institutions. He noted that administrative integration is also occurring in Hasakah province. Abdi said the integration of SDF fighters and military commanders into the army reflects a degree of trust in the Syrian state, though he stressed that further practical steps are required to build full confidence. He described the process as having the backing of the Syrian state and several countries, noting a shared commitment to moving forward despite existing obstacles."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "US annual inflation rate rose to 3.7% in September",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "According to data from the Bureau of Labor Statistics released on Wednesday, US price increases reached an annual rate of 3.7% in September, up from 3.4% in August. This represents the highest inflation rate since the annual rate reached a three-year peak of 4.2% in May. The report comes ahead of the midterm elections and amid polls showing widespread dissatisfaction with the Trump administration regarding the cost of living. The Guardian World reports that price increases this year have been largely driven by the US-Israel war with Iran, which has caused energy prices to rise. Gasoline accounted for over half of overall price increases, rising 34% from last year, while fuel oil increased by 74% from last year. Additionally, air travel increased by 22.4%."
    },
    {
      "text": "Overall consumer prices rose 0.6% from August. Core inflation, which excludes volatile food and energy prices, increased by 0.3% in September to an annual rate of 2.6%. Data from AAA indicates that average gas prices remain more than a dollar per gallon higher than last year, while diesel reached a record high last month."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "13th Financial Times Africa Summit scheduled for London",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The 13th Financial Times (FT) Africa Summit is expected to take place at The Landmark in London, according to a statement issued in Accra. The event, themed “Mobilising Growth in a New Global Order,” is scheduled to bring together African leaders, policymakers, investors, and business executives to discuss the continent's economic future. Expected attendees include President John Dramani Mahama and former Nigerian President Olusegun Obasanjo. Other confirmed speakers include Aliko Dangote, President and Chief Executive of the Dangote Group; Jakaya Kikwete, former President of Tanzania; Kristy McNeill, the United Kingdom’s Minister for International Development; Mo Ibrahim, founder of the Mo Ibrahim Foundation; and Ahmed Shide, Ethiopia’s Minister of Finance. Ministers and business leaders from South Africa, Nigeria, Ethiopia, Angola, Mauritius, Rwanda, Sierra Leone, Côte d’Ivoire, and the Democratic Republic of the Congo are also expected to participate. The summit is scheduled to run from October 21 to 22, 2026."
    },
    {
      "text": "The two-day summit will feature discussions on finance, trade, technology, infrastructure, energy, and industrial development, with an emphasis on attracting investment and building domestic markets. The first day is planned to open with a keynote interview with President Mahama, followed by sessions regarding the future of finance, domestic capital mobilisation, African financial technology, small and medium-sized enterprise expansion, gold reserve management, and the economic implications of rapid urbanisation. The second day is scheduled to examine how policy reforms could support sustainable and inclusive growth, and how capital and investment risks might align with Africa’s development ambitions. Participants are also expected to discuss strengthening African trade amid shifting global alliances, rising tariffs, the implications of the Red Sea corridor, and United States policy towards Africa."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Man killed in Chapainawabganj, Bangladesh road collision",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "A man was killed on a Wednesday afternoon following a collision between a truck and a CNG-run autorickshaw in Shibganj upazila of Chapainawabganj, Bangladesh, according to reports from The Financial Express Bangladesh and UNB. The deceased was identified as 32-year-old Ajim Uddin, son of Hasanul, from the Chouka area of the upazila. Ajim was a passenger in the autorickshaw at the time of the accident. Local residents rescued him and transported him to Sadar hospital, where an attending doctor declared him dead after he sustained critical injuries. Quoting locals, police stated the accident occurred in the Chhatrajitpur-Phultala area when the autorickshaw, traveling from Chapainawabganj, collided with a stone-laden truck coming from the Sonamasjid Land Port. Matiur Rahman, the officer-in-charge (OC) of Shibganj Police Station, said that while police seized the truck, the driver fled the scene. Rahman added that legal action would be taken in connection with the accident."
    }
  ]
}
